interviewQuestions.answerMarkdown:
I'd start from deny-everything and add only what the app provably needs:

```
default-src 'self';
script-src 'nonce-{perResponse}' 'strict-dynamic';
style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';
img-src 'self' data: https://cdn.example.com;
connect-src 'self' https://api.example.com https://analytics.vendor.com;
object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self';
frame-ancestors 'none'; report-uri /csp-report
```

**Why nonce rather than a host allowlist.** Allowlisting a popular CDN is usually equivalent to allowing arbitrary script, because those hosts often serve JSONP endpoints or old library builds that execute caller-supplied code — large-scale analyses found most allowlist policies bypassable this way. `'strict-dynamic'` then lets the analytics loader pull its own scripts without me enumerating them.

**Deliberate concessions.** `'unsafe-inline'` in `style-src`, because CSS-in-JS requires it and inline style is far lower risk than inline script. I would not make the same concession in `script-src`.

**Rollout.** Report-only for two weeks, filter out the browser-extension noise that will dominate the reports, fix our own violations with nonces rather than by widening rules, then enforce on a traffic slice. Both headers can be sent at once, so I keep report-only running for the next, stricter policy.

**Testing:** whether you can build a policy from the app's actual dependency graph and defend each relaxation, rather than reciting directives.
